#! mathcas 2 notebook

#% title
# array_reduce

#% text
# `array_reduce(f, a, n)` applies `f` to each vector of the array `a` along level `n`; the result is the array without that level.

#% section
# Details

#% item
# `array_reduce(f, a, [n1, n2, ...])` applies `f` to each sub-array along the levels `n1, n2, ...`, its levels in that order.

#% item
# A negative level counts from the deepest one.

#% section
# Examples

#% subsection
# Basic examples

#% cell
array_reduce(total, [[1, 2, 3], [4, 5, 6]], 1)
#% output
#= [5, 7, 9]

#% cell
array_reduce(total, [[1, 2, 3], [4, 5, 6]], 2)
#% output
#= [6, 15]

#% cell
array_reduce(f, [[[1, 2], [3, 4]], [[5, 6], [7, 8]]], [1, 3])
#% output
#= [f([[1, 2], [5, 6]]), f([[3, 4], [7, 8]])]

#% cell
array_reduce(apply(h), [[a, b], [c, d]], -1)
#% output
#= [h(a, b), h(c, d)]

#% section
# See also

#% text
# `array_filter`, `total`, `transpose`
